# Sunfront Garrison — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Sunfront Garrison, a low, wide fortified infantry
barracks — a muster post from which fresh troopers deploy — as a 3D voxel model with a
small rig that animates on its own at runtime. There is no target model to copy: build
something that reads unmistakably as this barracks building and satisfies the animation
contract below.

This brief fixes what the garrison is and how it must move. It does not give you a parts
list, joint placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a fortified barracks needs,
where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## How the tool works

`voxel-anim` paints discrete opaque cells into a voxel volume, one recorded operation at a
time. Place cells with `set-voxel`/`fill-box`/`stroke-box`/`line`/`add-sphere` (each an
opaque `#rrggbb` color) and clear them with the matching clear/erase ops; `mirror`
reflects the field across a plane. Global `--part <name>` selects the part an op sculpts,
and each part is sculpted separately in its own preview and log; create a part with
`define-part` before you sculpt into it.

A call **records only** and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw
`parts/<part>.png` and the assembled `scene/*.png`, then read them between calls.
`voxel-anim --help` is the contract.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It starts
  empty.
- x runs across the barracks, 0–{{voxel.maxX}}. y runs up, 0 (the ground) to {{voxel.maxY}} (top). z runs
  front-to-back, 0–{{voxel.maxZ}}. Forward is +z: the front face — with the muster gate — is toward z = {{voxel.maxZ}}. Up is
  +y.
- Build the barracks symmetric about the vertical centerplane; the left and right halves
  mirror each other. The muster gate, its ramp, and the belfry sit on the centerline.
- The garrison is low and wide — a broad, squat fortified blockhouse that hugs the ground,
  not a tall tower or spire. The blockhouse body fills most of the length and width and
  rises only partway up the volume; the belfry over the gate reaches higher.

## What the Garrison is (and what is yours to invent)

The building must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A low, wide **fortified blockhouse barracks** of brass-and-sandstone masonry — a squat,
  broad stronghold that hugs the ground, with a **crenellated parapet** along its roofline
  and **slit windows / firing ports** in its walls so it reads as manned infantry quarters,
  not a plain box, a tall tower, or an industrial foundry.
- A fortified **muster gate** set into the front face, with a **deployment ramp** — a
  hinged gangway at the gate's sill — that troopers march down when they deploy.
- A small **belfry** (a bell-cote or turret) rising over the gate, holding an iron **muster
  bell** hung from a yoke.
- A clear solar-amber energy accent and the palette below.

Everything else is yours to invent — the silhouette and proportions, how the blockhouse is
tiered and buttressed, how the parapet and firing ports are arranged, how the gate, ramp,
belfry, and bell are shaped, and how you break the building into rig parts and place its
joints. The test rewards a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the Garrison —
a humble, fortified infantry billet, plainly different from the faction's foundries and
its grand Bastion keep — and animates convincingly.

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Masonry — primary plating (brass) | `#c69a4b` |
| Masonry — dark plating, underside, shadow (bronze) | `#7a5527` |
| Secondary panels / lighter structure (sandstone) | `#d9c48c` |
| Mechanisms, gate frame, ramp hinges, the bell (iron) | `#565c64` |
| Energy accent (solar amber) | `#ff9d2e` |
| Glowing core highlight (solar hot) | `#ffd76b` |

The solar-amber accent is the team-tint region: give the garrison a clear amber glow at
the muster gate and in the slit windows / firing ports (with solar-hot at the brightest
points), so the accent reads from multiple angles.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Both are self-playing decorative idles (`auto_play`, looping): the garrison
cycles continuously on its own with no caller. Author each with `voxel-anim
define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the period and interpolation so the motion
carries weight and every loop is seamless.

- **`muster_ramp_drop`** — the front deployment ramp hinges **down** from the muster gate's
  sill toward the ground and lifts back up each cycle, pivoting on its hinge like a
  drawbridge or gangway; the blockhouse holds still.
- **`muster_bell_swing`** — the belfry bell swings side to side about its yoke each cycle,
  like a pendulum ringing; the blockhouse holds still.

You may add extra parts, joints, and auto-play animations of your own; you must produce
these two animations, by these names, and must not contradict them.

## Working the tool

Define your parts with `define-part`, sculpt each with `--part <name>`, set pivots
with `set-pivot`, place joints with `define-joint`, and author the two animations'
keyframes — running `voxel-anim render` and reading `parts/<part>.png` and the
`scene/*.png` previews between calls to confirm the parts fit, the ramp seats into the
gate's sill on its hinge, the bell hangs in the belfry, and the animations read with
weight. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available
operations, the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish
so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part
scores as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim
render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check
the motion). The recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your submission.
